#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Red, then green (board TI, item 1; docs/TEST_INTEGRITY.md): each test function a commit adds,
or whose body it changes, is run against the commit's PARENT, the commit's tests in place and
the parent's code, and must not pass there. A test that never failed cannot be shown to detect
its fault.

    scripts/nmas-red-green [--repo DIR] [--commit SHA]          # one commit (default HEAD)
    scripts/nmas-red-green [--repo DIR] --message FILE          # the commit being made: the
                                                                # index against HEAD
    scripts/nmas-red-green [--repo DIR] --commit SHA --message FILE   # that commit, the
                                                                # trailers read from FILE
    scripts/nmas-red-green [--repo DIR] --range A..B            # each commit in the range (CI)
        [--plan-only] [--allow-unconfined] [--python PY]

**Which tests.** Each changed `tests/**/test_*.py` (the commit's pytest.ini's `python_files`,
`python_classes`, `python_functions`, or pytest's defaults) outside the DATA folders the
commit's tests/data_folders.py declares is PARSED at the commit and at its parent (`ast`),
never read from a diff's text. **A data folder is never Mercury's tests** (C2633, the operator,
2026-11-06): the job gate's fixture jobs hold their own `test_*.py`, failing on purpose; the
declaration is PARSED at the commit (never imported), the same tuple tests/conftest.py's
collection reads, and an entry outside `tests/fixtures/` is REFUSED, naming it, so a
declaration cannot hide Mercury's own tests. A test function is identified as its pytest node
id, `path::name` or `path::Class::name` (nested classes too). One is selected when it is new at
the commit, or when its shape changed: its `ast.dump` (no positions), its decorators and
arguments included, its leading docstring left out (words about a test assert nothing), so a
comment, a docstring or a move within the file selects nothing. A function new at its id whose
shape is that of a function the commit took away from another id (a move to another file or
class, a file renamed) is a MOVE and is not selected. Not seen: a test whose own text is
unchanged while a helper, fixture, conftest or class-level mark it uses changed; a test defined
inside an `if` at module level.

**The parent.** A throwaway worktree of the parent (`git worktree add --detach`, as
scripts/nmas-control makes one), in a fresh temporary folder outside the checkout, removed
afterwards (`git worktree remove --force`, then `git worktree prune`) whatever happened,
SIGTERM and SIGHUP included. Its `tests/` is removed and the COMMIT's whole `tests/` put in its
place (each file of `git ls-tree -r`, or of the index's `git ls-files -s`, read by one
`git cat-file --batch`; a path or a link reaching outside the folder refused), and staged in
the worktree's own index, so a scan
through `tests.source_index.tracked()` lists the commit's tests: the test side new, the code
side old. Nothing is written to the checkout.

**The run.** Through scripts/nmas-test (confined to loopback, CI's interpreter or PYTHON; with
`--allow-unconfined` where no namespace can be made, which nmas-test says), in the worktree,
`-p no:randomly -p no:cacheprovider`, serial (no `-n`: scripts/nmas-test turns `-n 0` into its
memory's worker count, because scripts/nmas-workers takes `--cap 0` as no cap, and xdist given
no `-n` does not distribute), and the outcomes read from pytest's JUnit XML (xunit2), parsed:
1. a collection (`--collect-only -q --continue-on-collection-errors`) of the changed files: the
   files that do not collect on the parent, and the node ids that do;
2. a run of exactly the selected functions' node ids that collected (pytest expands a
   parametrized function's cases).

**The rules** (D1, as the operator changed it on 2026-11-06, C2633: "a collection or setup
error on the parent never counts as red: each new test must fail on the parent by its own
assertion", read as the test's own BODY failing). A test is RED on the parent when its body ran
there and raised (pytest's JUnit `failure`: an assertion, or a name the parent lacks); a setup
or teardown error (JUnit's `error`) is NOT red, since the body never ran or already passed.
**A parametrized function is red when at least one of its cases failed**; otherwise it is
"error" if any case errored, then passed if any passed. Then:
- red: passes the check;
- error, passed, skipped, xfailed, or not collected (in a file that collected): REFUSED, unless
  the message carries `Guards-Existing: <test id> — <reason>` (a test written to hold behaviour
  that already works, such as a regression net before a refactor);
- in a file that does not collect on the parent at all: reported APART, since it shows nothing
  about any one test. **`Red-By-Collection: <tests/file.py> — <reason>` excuses it only when
  that file's collection error on the parent is an ImportError or ModuleNotFoundError naming a
  module this commit ADDS** (the operator, 2026-11-06), checked against the commit's own added
  paths (`No module named 'M'` with M added, or `cannot import name 'X' from 'M'` with M.X
  added). Any other collection error (a fixture, a syntax error, a name added to a module that
  already exists) is REFUSED whatever the trailer, naming the error;
- behind a conftest that does not load on the parent: REFUSED, naming the conftest and its
  error; no trailer excuses it.
A trailer is an em dash, en dash or hyphen between the name and the reason, spaced; one without
its reason excuses nothing and is refused. Each refusal names the test, its outcome on the
parent against the red it needed, the parent's sha, and the trailer that would excuse it.

**Limit** (the board's): red on the parent says the test CAN fail; whether it failed for the
right reason (a renamed function, a missing fixture file) is the reviewer's. Each red test's
first failure line is printed for that reading.

**The index** (`--message` without `--commit`): the default index, read without taking its
lock or writing an object (`git diff-index --cached`, `git show :<path>`, `git ls-files -s`;
`git write-tree` takes the lock, and fails while another git holds it); a `git commit -a` or
`git commit <path>` builds a temporary index (GIT_INDEX_FILE), which this refuses to guess at
(exit 2). The gate stages with `git add -A` before it commits.

Output: per commit, one line per selected test and a summary: `N selected, R red, E excused,
F refused`, with how many of them are in files that do not collect. A commit with no test
function added or changed passes and says so; a root commit is said and passed (no parent).
Exit 0 every selected test red or excused; 1 refused; 2 could not ask (git, or a run that gave
no report), which is never a pass.
"""
import argparse
import ast
import collections
import configparser
import fnmatch
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, REPO)
# Phase 4 section 8.1: the interpreter flask-app runs, before any other import.
from modules.app_interpreter import adopt  # noqa: E402
adopt(__name__)

NMAS_TEST = os.path.join(REPO, "scripts", "nmas-test")
#: pytest's defaults where the commit has no pytest.ini or the ini does not set one.
DEFAULT_PATTERNS = {"python_files": "test_*.py *_test.py", "python_classes": "Test",
                    "python_functions": "test"}
#: Red is the test's own body failing (C2633): JUnit's `failure`, never its `error` (setup or
#: teardown).
RED = ("failed",)
#: The one declaration of the folders under tests/ that hold data (C2633), and where each entry
#: must lie.
DATA_FOLDERS_FILE = "tests/data_folders.py"
DATA_FOLDERS_NAME = "DATA_FOLDERS"
DATA_ROOT = "tests/fixtures/"
#: A collection error's words that name the module it could not import.
NO_MODULE = re.compile(r"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '(?P<module>[\w.]+)'")
NO_NAME = re.compile(r"ImportError: cannot import name '(?P<name>\w+)' from '(?P<module>[\w.]+)'")
#: The commit being made: the default index, its own side.
INDEX = ":"
#: One git read's bound (repo.run_git's own default). The reads here are a `git show` of one
#: file or a `diff-tree` of one commit; the whole selection of the last three commits took
#: under 0.5 s (2026-11-03, --plan-only). A call that hits it names itself (exit 2).
GIT_TIMEOUT = 30
#: The worktree's add and the read of tests/ (measured 2026-11-03 on the laptop, this
#: repository: 0.33 s, and 0.11 s for `git archive` of tests/, which one `cat-file --batch`
#: of the same blobs matches). A CI runner's were not measured, so the bound is generous
#: rather than 2.5 times, and a call that hits it names itself (exit 2), never a pass.
WORKTREE_TIMEOUT = 60
#: scripts/nmas-test bounds each of its runs itself (NMAS_TEST_TIMEOUT, 300 s by default, the
#: suite's per-process bound) and needs up to 15 s more to end what it started and report:
#: this is the backstop beyond that, never the bound that is meant to fire.
RUNNER_SLACK = 60
#: Variables that would carry the calling run's pytest or coverage configuration into the run.
DROP_ENV = ("PYTEST_ADDOPTS", "PYTEST_CURRENT_TEST", "PYTEST_XDIST_WORKER",
            "PYTEST_XDIST_WORKER_COUNT", "PYTEST_XDIST_TESTRUNUID", "PYTEST_PLUGINS")
TRAILER_KEYS = ("Guards-Existing", "Red-By-Collection")
ANY_KEY = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z]+(?:-[A-Za-z]+)*:\s")
TRAILER_VALUE = re.compile(r"^(?P<subject>\S+)\s+(?:—|–|-{1,2})\s+(?P<reason>\S.*)$", re.S)
SCISSORS = re.compile(r"^# -+ >8 -+$", re.M)
#: pytest's own line when a conftest does not import (it then writes no report at all).
CONFTEST_FAILED = re.compile(r"^ImportError while loading conftest '(?P<path>[^']+)'\.$", re.M)


class CannotAsk(Exception):
    pass


def _exit_on_signal(signum, _frame):
    """SIGTERM and SIGHUP end the run as an exit does, so its `finally` removes the worktree."""
    raise SystemExit(128 + signum)


def git(root, *args, text=True, timeout=GIT_TIMEOUT, ok_missing=False, input=None):
    """git in *root*, through the one runner pinned to it (C931, `repo.run_git`)."""
    from modules.nsot.repo import run_git
    try:
        r = run_git(root, *args, text=text, timeout=timeout, input=input)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        raise CannotAsk(f"git {' '.join(args)}: {exc}") from exc
    if r.returncode != 0:
        if ok_missing:
            return None
        err = r.stderr if text else r.stderr.decode(errors="replace")
        raise CannotAsk(f"git {' '.join(args)} exited {r.returncode}: {err.strip()[:300]}")
    return r.stdout


# --- which tests ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

def patterns(ini_text):
    """`{option: [pattern]}` for python_files, python_classes and python_functions, from a
    pytest.ini's text (or pytest's defaults)."""
    got = dict(DEFAULT_PATTERNS)
    if ini_text:
        cp = configparser.ConfigParser(interpolation=None)
        try:
            cp.read_string(ini_text)
        except configparser.Error:
            cp = None
        if cp is not None and cp.has_section("pytest"):
            for key in got:
                if cp.has_option("pytest", key):
                    got[key] = cp.get("pytest", key)
    return {k: v.split() for k, v in got.items()}


def _matches(name, pats, glob_only=False):
    """pytest's matching: a pattern holding a glob character is matched with fnmatch, any other
    as a prefix (python_files is always a glob)."""
    for p in pats:
        if glob_only or any(c in p for c in "*?["):
            if fnmatch.fnmatchcase(name, p):
                return True
        elif name.startswith(p):
            return True
    return False


def data_folders(text):
    """`(folders, problems)`: tests/data_folders.py's `DATA_FOLDERS` at one commit, PARSED
    (never imported). No file: no folders. Each entry must be a literal string under
    `tests/fixtures/` ending in "/", with no `..`; one that is not is a problem, named, and
    declares nothing."""
    if text is None:
        return (), []
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(text)
    except (SyntaxError, ValueError) as exc:
        return (), [f"{DATA_FOLDERS_FILE} does not parse ({exc}): it declares nothing"]
    found = [n for n in tree.body if isinstance(n, ast.Assign) and any(
        isinstance(t, ast.Name) and t.id == DATA_FOLDERS_NAME for t in n.targets)]
    if len(found) != 1 or not isinstance(found[0].value, (ast.Tuple, ast.List)):
        return (), [f"{DATA_FOLDERS_FILE} holds {len(found)} literal {DATA_FOLDERS_NAME} "
                    "tuple(s), where one was required: it declares nothing"]
    folders, problems = [], []
    for e in found[0].value.elts:
        v = e.value if isinstance(e, ast.Constant) and isinstance(e.value, str) else None
        if v is None or not v.startswith(DATA_ROOT) or not v.endswith("/") or \
                ".." in v.split("/") or v == DATA_ROOT:
            problems.append(f"{DATA_FOLDERS_FILE} declares {ast.unparse(e)} a data folder: an "
                            f"entry must be a folder under {DATA_ROOT} ending in '/', so "
                            "Mercury's own tests stay judged")
        else:
            folders.append(v)
    return tuple(folders), problems


def is_test_file(path, pats):
    return bool(path) and path.startswith("tests/") and path.endswith(".py") and \
        not path.startswith(tuple(pats.get("data", ()))) and \
        _matches(path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1], pats["python_files"], glob_only=True)


def added_modules(changes):
    """The dotted modules the commit ADDS on the code side (a `.py` path new at the commit, a
    package by its `__init__.py`). Paths under tests/ are left out: the parent's worktree holds
    the commit's whole tests/, so none of them can be what the parent lacks."""
    out = set()
    for old, new in changes:
        if old is None and new and new.endswith(".py") and not new.startswith("tests/"):
            dotted = new[:-3].replace("/", ".")
            out.add(dotted[:-len(".__init__")] if dotted.endswith(".__init__") else dotted)
    return out


def import_of_an_added_module(error_text, added):
    """The module an ImportError or ModuleNotFoundError in *error_text* names, when the commit
    adds it, else ""."""
    for m in NO_MODULE.finditer(error_text or ""):
        if m.group("module") in added:
            return m.group("module")
    for m in NO_NAME.finditer(error_text or ""):
        dotted = f"{m.group('module')}.{m.group('name')}"
        if dotted in added:
            return dotted
    return ""


def _error_line(text):
    """The error's own line: pytest's `E   ...` line, else its last non-empty line."""
    lines = [ln.strip() for ln in (text or "").splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    marked = [ln[1:].strip() for ln in lines if ln.startswith("E ")]
    return (marked[-1] if marked else lines[-1] if lines else "collection failure")[:200]


def _is_docstring(stmt):
    return (isinstance(stmt, ast.Expr) and isinstance(stmt.value, ast.Constant)
            and isinstance(stmt.value.value, str))


def shape(node):
    """A function's shape: its kind, arguments, decorators, return annotation and body, each
    `ast.dump`ed (no positions), its leading docstring left out."""
    body = node.body[1:] if node.body and _is_docstring(node.body[0]) else node.body
    return (type(node).__name__, ast.dump(node.args),
            tuple(ast.dump(d) for d in node.decorator_list),
            ast.dump(node.returns) if node.returns is not None else "",
            tuple(ast.dump(s) for s in body))


def test_functions(source, pats):
    """`{qualified id: shape}` for what pytest collects in *source* (module-level functions and
    the methods of matching classes, nested classes included, joined by `::`), or None when
    *source* does not parse."""
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(source)
    except (SyntaxError, ValueError):
        return None
    out = {}

    def walk(body, prefix):
        for node in body:
            if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)) and \
                    _matches(node.name, pats["python_functions"]):
                out[prefix + node.name] = shape(node)
            elif isinstance(node, ast.ClassDef) and _matches(node.name, pats["python_classes"]):
                walk(node.body, f"{prefix}{node.name}::")

    walk(tree.body, "")
    return out


def show(root, side, path):
    """*path*'s text at *side* (a commit, or INDEX), or None where it holds none."""
    return git(root, "show", f"{'' if side == INDEX else side}:{path}", ok_missing=True)


def changes_between(root, parent, commit):
    """`[(old path or None, new path or None)]` from `git diff-tree -r -M --name-status -z`
    (or `git diff-index --cached`, the same format, for the index)."""
    if commit == INDEX:
        text = git(root, "diff-index", "--cached", "-M", "--name-status", "-z", parent)
    else:
        text = git(root, "diff-tree", "-r", "-M", "--name-status", "-z", parent, commit)
    parts = text.split("\0")
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(parts) and parts[i]:
        status = parts[i]
        if status[0] in "RC":
            old, new = parts[i + 1], parts[i + 2]
            out.append((old if status[0] == "R" else None, new))
            i += 3
        else:
            path = parts[i + 1]
            out.append((None if status[0] == "A" else path, None if status[0] == "D" else path))
            i += 2
    return out


def select(changes, read_parent, read_commit, pats):
    """`(selected, notes)`: selected is `[(id, "added" | "changed")]` in file order, notes the
    changed test files that do not parse at the commit (nothing in them can be judged)."""
    before, after, notes = {}, {}, []
    for old, new in changes:
        if is_test_file(old, pats):
            funcs = test_functions(read_parent(old) or "", pats) or {}
            before.update({f"{old}::{q}": s for q, s in funcs.items()})
        if is_test_file(new, pats):
            funcs = test_functions(read_commit(new) or "", pats)
            if funcs is None:
                notes.append(f"{new} does not parse at the commit: none of its tests is judged")
                continue
            after.update({f"{new}::{q}": s for q, s in funcs.items()})
    gone = collections.Counter(s for k, s in before.items() if k not in after)
    selected = []
    for k, s in after.items():
        if k in before:
            if before[k] != s:
                selected.append((k, "changed"))
        elif gone[s] > 0:
            gone[s] -= 1          # a move: the same shape the commit took from another id
        else:
            selected.append((k, "added"))
    return selected, notes


# --- trailers -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def message_body(message):
    cut = SCISSORS.search(message)
    if cut:
        message = message[:cut.start()]
    return "\n".join(line for line in message.splitlines() if not line.startswith("#"))


def trailer_values(message, key):
    """Each `key:` trailer's value, a wrapped value joined: it runs to a blank line or the next
    `Key:` line."""
    head = re.compile(rf"^{re.escape(key)}:[ \t]*", re.I)
    out, cur = [], None
    for line in message_body(message).splitlines():
        if head.match(line):
            if cur is not None:
                out.append(cur)
            cur = head.sub("", line).strip()
        elif cur is not None:
            if not line.strip() or ANY_KEY.match(line):
                out.append(cur)
                cur = None
            else:
                cur += " " + line.strip()
    if cur is not None:
        out.append(cur)
    return out


def excuses(message):
    """`({key: {name: reason}}, [malformed trailer])`."""
    got, bad = {k: {} for k in TRAILER_KEYS}, []
    for key in TRAILER_KEYS:
        for value in trailer_values(message, key):
            m = TRAILER_VALUE.match(value)
            if not m:
                bad.append(f"a {key} trailer without its ' — <reason>' (\"{value[:100]}\")")
                continue
            got[key][m.group("subject")] = m.group("reason")
    return got, bad


# --- the parent's run -----------------------------------------------------------------------

def _dotted(path):
    """pytest's JUnit name for a test file: the path without `.py`, `/` made `.`."""
    return re.sub(r"\.py$", "", path).replace("/", ".")


def junit_cases(xml_path):
    """`[(classname, name, outcome, message, text)]` from a JUnit XML file, outcome one of
    passed, failed, error, skipped, xfailed, *text* the element's own text (a collection
    error's traceback); None when the file is absent or does not parse."""
    try:
        tree = ET.parse(xml_path)
    except (OSError, ET.ParseError):
        return None
    out = []
    for case in tree.iter("testcase"):
        outcome, message, text = "passed", "", ""
        for child in case:
            if child.tag in ("failure", "error"):
                outcome = "failed" if child.tag == "failure" else "error"
                message, text = child.get("message") or "", child.text or ""
                break
            if child.tag == "skipped":
                outcome = "xfailed" if child.get("type") == "pytest.xfail" else "skipped"
                message = child.get("message") or ""
        out.append((case.get("classname") or "", case.get("name") or "", outcome, message,
                    text))
    return out


def child_env(python):
    """The run's environment: the calling run's pytest and coverage settings dropped, and git's
    repository-local variables too (`repo.REPO_LOCAL_ENV`). As a commit-msg hook this runs with
    git's GIT_DIR and GIT_INDEX_FILE set, and a test's own `git init` in its temporary folder
    then reinitialised the repository they name, writing `core.bare = true` into the
    checkout's config (C2357, 2026-11-03)."""
    from modules.nsot.repo import REPO_LOCAL_ENV
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items()
           if k not in DROP_ENV and k not in REPO_LOCAL_ENV and not k.startswith("COV_CORE_")}
    env["PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE"] = "1"
    if python:
        env["PYTHON"] = python
    return env


def runner_timeout():
    try:
        return float(os.environ.get("NMAS_TEST_TIMEOUT") or 300) + RUNNER_SLACK
    except ValueError:
        return 300 + RUNNER_SLACK


def nmas_test(tree, args, allow_unconfined, python):
    cmd = [NMAS_TEST, *(["--allow-unconfined"] if allow_unconfined else []), *args]
    try:
        return subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=tree, env=child_env(python), stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=runner_timeout())
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as exc:
        raise CannotAsk(f"scripts/nmas-test ran past {runner_timeout():.0f} s "
                        f"(NMAS_TEST_TIMEOUT plus {RUNNER_SLACK} s): {' '.join(args[-3:])}") \
            from exc


def _tail(proc, n=6):
    lines = [x for x in (proc.stdout + proc.stderr).splitlines() if x.strip()]
    return " | ".join(lines[-n:])[-600:]


def tests_entries(root, side):
    """`[(mode, blob, path)]` for every file under tests/ at *side*: `git ls-tree -r` of a
    commit, `git ls-files -s` of the index (neither takes the index's lock)."""
    out = []
    if side == INDEX:
        for rec in filter(None, git(root, "ls-files", "-s", "-z", "--", "tests").split("\0")):
            meta, path = rec.split("\t", 1)
            mode, blob, stage = meta.split()
            if stage != "0":
                raise CannotAsk(f"{path} is unmerged in the index (stage {stage})")
            out.append((mode, blob, path))
    else:
        for rec in filter(None, git(root, "ls-tree", "-r", "-z", side, "--", "tests")
                          .split("\0")):
            meta, path = rec.split("\t", 1)
            mode, _kind, blob = meta.split()
            out.append((mode, blob, path))
    return [e for e in out if e[0] != "160000"]          # a submodule holds no file here


def write_tests(root, side, tree):
    """*side*'s tests/ written into *tree*, each blob read by one `git cat-file --batch`; a
    path or a link reaching outside *tree* is refused, never written."""
    entries = tests_entries(root, side)
    if not entries:
        return 0
    data = git(root, "cat-file", "--batch", text=False, timeout=WORKTREE_TIMEOUT,
               input=("\n".join(b for _m, b, _p in entries) + "\n").encode())
    base = os.path.realpath(tree)

    def within(p):
        p = os.path.realpath(p)
        return p == base or p.startswith(base + os.sep)

    pos = 0
    for mode, blob, path in entries:
        nl = data.index(b"\n", pos)
        head = data[pos:nl].split()
        if len(head) != 3 or head[0].decode() != blob:
            raise CannotAsk(f"git cat-file gave {data[pos:nl][:80]!r} for {path}'s {blob}")
        size = int(head[2])
        body = data[nl + 1:nl + 1 + size]
        pos = nl + 1 + size + 1
        dest = os.path.join(tree, *path.split("/"))
        if not within(os.path.dirname(dest)):
            raise CannotAsk(f"{path} reaches outside the worktree")
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dest), exist_ok=True)
        if mode == "120000":
            target = body.decode("utf-8", errors="surrogateescape")
            if os.path.isabs(target) or not within(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(dest), target)):
                raise CannotAsk(f"{path} links outside the worktree ({target})")
            os.symlink(target, dest)
        else:
            with open(dest, "wb") as fh:
                fh.write(body)
            os.chmod(dest, 0o755 if mode == "100755" else 0o644)
    return len(entries)


def make_parent_tree(root, parent, commit, tmp):
    """The parent's worktree with the commit's whole tests/ in place, staged in its index."""
    tree = os.path.join(tmp, "tree")
    git(root, "worktree", "add", "--detach", "--quiet", tree, parent, timeout=WORKTREE_TIMEOUT)
    shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(tree, "tests"), ignore_errors=True)
    write_tests(root, commit, tree)
    git(tree, "add", "-A", "--", "tests")
    return tree


def remove_tree(root, tmp):
    tree = os.path.join(tmp, "tree")
    if os.path.exists(tree):
        git(root, "worktree", "remove", "--force", tree, ok_missing=True,
            timeout=WORKTREE_TIMEOUT)
    shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    git(root, "worktree", "prune", ok_missing=True)


def _relative(path, tree):
    """*path* (pytest's, absolute) relative to the worktree, whichever spelling of it pytest
    used (the temporary folder may sit behind a link)."""
    if not os.path.isabs(path):
        return path
    for base in (tree, os.path.realpath(tree)):
        rel = os.path.relpath(path, base)
        if not rel.startswith(".."):
            return rel
    return path


def run_on_parent(root, parent, commit, ids, allow_unconfined=False, python=None):
    """`{"collection": {file: message}, "conftest": path or None, "outcomes": {id: (outcome,
    [case lines])}}` for the function ids *ids*, run in the parent's worktree with the commit's
    tests."""
    files = sorted({i.split("::", 1)[0] for i in ids})
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="nmas-red-green-")
    try:
        tree = make_parent_tree(root, parent, commit, tmp)

        def clean(msg):
            """A failure's words with the throwaway folder's path read as `<parent>`."""
            for base in sorted({os.path.realpath(tree), tree}, key=len, reverse=True):
                msg = msg.replace(base, "<parent>")
            return msg

        collect_xml = os.path.join(tmp, "collect.xml")
        common = ["-p", "no:randomly", "-p", "no:cacheprovider", "-o", "junit_family=xunit2"]
        c = nmas_test(tree, ["--collect-only", "-q", *common, "--continue-on-collection-errors",
                             f"--junitxml={collect_xml}", *files], allow_unconfined, python)
        cases = junit_cases(collect_xml)
        if cases is None:
            out = c.stdout + c.stderr
            m = CONFTEST_FAILED.search(out)
            if m:
                conftest = _relative(m.group("path"), tree)
                error = clean(_error_line(out[m.end():]))
                why = f"{conftest} does not load on the parent ({error})"
                return {"collection": {f: why for f in files}, "conftest": conftest,
                        "outcomes": {}}
            raise CannotAsk(f"the parent's collection gave no report (scripts/nmas-test exit "
                            f"{c.returncode}): {_tail(c)}")
        by_dotted = {_dotted(f): f for f in files}
        collection = {}
        for classname, name, outcome, message, text in cases:
            # A collection error is JUnit's `error` on a case named for the file.
            if not classname and name in by_dotted and outcome in ("failed", "error"):
                collection[by_dotted[name]] = clean(text or message) or "collection failure"
        prefixes = tuple(f + "::" for f in files)
        collected = [line.strip() for line in c.stdout.splitlines()
                     if line.strip().startswith(prefixes)]
        runnable = [i for i in ids if i.split("::", 1)[0] not in collection and any(
            x == i or x.startswith(i + "[") for x in collected)]
        outcomes = {i: ("not collected", []) for i in ids
                    if i.split("::", 1)[0] not in collection and i not in runnable}
        if runnable:
            run_xml = os.path.join(tmp, "run.xml")
            r = nmas_test(tree, ["-q", *common, f"--junitxml={run_xml}", *runnable],
                          allow_unconfined, python)
            got = junit_cases(run_xml)
            if got is None:
                raise CannotAsk(f"the parent's run gave no report (scripts/nmas-test exit "
                                f"{r.returncode}): {_tail(r)}")
            for i in runnable:
                path, rest = i.split("::", 1)
                parts = rest.split("::")
                classname = ".".join([_dotted(path), *parts[:-1]])
                mine = [(n, o, msg) for cn, n, o, msg, _text in got if cn == classname and
                        (n == parts[-1] or n.startswith(parts[-1] + "["))]
                outcomes[i] = (verdict([o for _n, o, _m in mine]),
                               [f"{n}: {o}" + (f" ({clean(msg).splitlines()[0][:120]})"
                                               if msg.strip() else "") for n, o, msg in mine])
        return {"collection": collection, "outcomes": outcomes, "conftest": None}
    finally:
        remove_tree(root, tmp)


def verdict(case_outcomes):
    """One function's outcome from its cases': failed (red) if ANY case failed; else error if
    any errored (not red, C2633); else passed if any passed; else xfailed, skipped, or "no
    outcome" when no case was reported."""
    for o in ("failed", "error", "passed", "xfailed", "skipped"):
        if o in case_outcomes:
            return o
    return "no outcome"


# --- the judgement --------------------------------------------------------------------------

def judge(selected, run, message, parent, added=frozenset()):
    """`(lines, counts, refused)` for one commit; *added* the dotted modules it adds."""
    ex, bad = excuses(message)
    guards, by_collection = ex["Guards-Existing"], ex["Red-By-Collection"]
    lines, refused = [], list(bad)
    counts = collections.Counter()
    p = parent[:12]
    for tid, why in selected:
        counts["selected"] += 1
        path = tid.split("::", 1)[0]
        if path in run["collection"]:
            counts["collection"] += 1
            conftest = run.get("conftest")
            text = run["collection"][path]
            error = text if conftest else _error_line(text)
            module = "" if conftest else import_of_an_added_module(text, added)
            if module and path in by_collection:
                counts["excused"] += 1
                lines.append(f"  excused  {tid} ({why}): {path} does not collect on the parent "
                             f"{p} ({error}), a module this commit adds ({module}); "
                             f"Red-By-Collection: {by_collection[path]}")
                continue
            counts["refused"] += 1
            if conftest:
                lines.append(f"  REFUSED  {tid} ({why}): {error}, which shows nothing about "
                             "this test; no trailer excuses a conftest that does not load")
                refused.append(f"{tid}: {error}; a conftest that does not load on the parent "
                               "is refused whatever the trailer (C2633)")
            elif module:
                lines.append(f"  REFUSED  {tid} ({why}): {path} does not collect on the parent "
                             f"{p} ({error}), which shows nothing about this test")
                refused.append(f"{tid}: {path} does not collect on the parent {p} ({error}, a "
                               f"module this commit adds: {module}); excuse with: "
                               f"Red-By-Collection: {path} — <reason>")
            else:
                lines.append(f"  REFUSED  {tid} ({why}): {path} does not collect on the parent "
                             f"{p} ({error}), which is not an ImportError naming a module this "
                             "commit adds, so no trailer excuses it")
                refused.append(f"{tid}: {path} does not collect on the parent {p} ({error}); "
                               "Red-By-Collection excuses only an ImportError or "
                               "ModuleNotFoundError naming a module this commit adds "
                               f"(added: {', '.join(sorted(added)) or 'none'}), C2633")
            continue
        outcome, cases = run["outcomes"].get(tid, ("no outcome", []))
        shown = "; ".join(cases[:4]) + (f"; and {len(cases) - 4} more" if len(cases) > 4 else "")
        if outcome in RED:
            counts["red"] += 1
            lines.append(f"  red      {tid} ({why}): {outcome} on the parent {p}"
                         + (f": {shown}" if shown else ""))
            if tid in guards:
                lines.append("           (Guards-Existing names it, and it is red: the trailer "
                             "is not needed)")
        elif tid in guards:
            counts["excused"] += 1
            lines.append(f"  excused  {tid} ({why}): {outcome} on the parent {p}; "
                         f"Guards-Existing: {guards[tid]}")
        else:
            counts["refused"] += 1
            said = (" (a setup or teardown error: the test's own body did not fail)"
                    if outcome == "error" else "")
            lines.append(f"  REFUSED  {tid} ({why}): {outcome} on the parent {p}{said}, where "
                         f"red (its own body failing) was required"
                         + (f": {shown}" if shown else ""))
            refused.append(f"{tid}: {outcome} on the parent {p}{said}, red (its own body "
                           f"failing) required; excuse with: Guards-Existing: {tid} — <reason>")
    return lines, counts, refused


def summary(counts):
    s = (f"{counts['selected']} selected, {counts['red']} red, {counts['excused']} excused, "
         f"{counts['refused']} refused")
    if counts["collection"]:
        s += f" ({counts['collection']} of them in files that do not collect on the parent)"
    return s


def check(root, parent, commit, message, label, plan_only=False, allow_unconfined=False,
          python=None):
    """`(lines, refused)` for one commit: *commit* a commit or tree, *parent* its parent."""
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    pats = patterns(show(root, commit, "pytest.ini"))
    folders, declared_wrong = data_folders(show(root, commit, DATA_FOLDERS_FILE))
    pats["data"] = folders
    changes = changes_between(root, parent, commit)
    selected, notes = select(changes, lambda p: show(root, parent, p),
                             lambda p: show(root, commit, p), pats)
    lines = [f"nmas-red-green: {label}"] + [f"  note: {n}" for n in notes]
    lines += [f"  REFUSED  {d}" for d in declared_wrong]
    if not selected:
        lines.append("  no test function added or changed: nothing to run, passes")
        return lines, list(declared_wrong)
    if plan_only:
        lines += [f"  selected {tid} ({why})" for tid, why in selected]
        lines.append(f"  {len(selected)} selected (--plan-only: nothing run)")
        return lines, list(declared_wrong)
    run = run_on_parent(root, parent, commit, [t for t, _w in selected], allow_unconfined,
                        python)
    more, counts, refused = judge(selected, run, message, parent, added_modules(changes))
    lines += more
    lines.append(f"  {summary(counts)}; {time.monotonic() - t0:.1f} s")
    return lines, list(declared_wrong) + refused


def _staged_index_ok(root):
    """The default index is the one this reads; a temporary one (GIT_INDEX_FILE) is refused."""
    given = os.environ.get("GIT_INDEX_FILE")
    if not given:
        return
    gitdir = git(root, "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir").strip()
    if os.path.realpath(os.path.join(root, given)) != os.path.realpath(
            os.path.join(gitdir, "index")):
        raise CannotAsk(f"GIT_INDEX_FILE is {given}, not {os.path.join(gitdir, 'index')}: the "
                        "commit being made is not the default index (a `git commit -a` or "
                        "`git commit <path>`), which this does not read")


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    ap.add_argument("--repo", default=os.getcwd(), help="the checkout (default: here)")
    g = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    g.add_argument("--commit", help="the commit to check (default HEAD)")
    g.add_argument("--range", help="A..B: each commit in it (CI)")
    ap.add_argument("--message", help="read the trailers from this file; without --commit, "
                    "the commit being made (the index against HEAD)")
    ap.add_argument("--plan-only", action="store_true", help="list the selected tests only")
    ap.add_argument("--allow-unconfined", action="store_true",
                    help="passed to scripts/nmas-test where no namespace can be made")
    ap.add_argument("--python", help="the interpreter scripts/nmas-test runs (PYTHON)")
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if a.range and a.message:
        ap.error("--message reads one commit's trailers: not with --range")
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP):
        signal.signal(sig, _exit_on_signal)
    root = os.path.abspath(a.repo)
    kw = {"plan_only": a.plan_only, "allow_unconfined": a.allow_unconfined, "python": a.python}
    out, refused = [], []
    try:
        if a.range:
            shas = git(root, "rev-list", "--no-merges", "--reverse", a.range).split()
            if not shas:
                out.append(f"nmas-red-green: {a.range} holds no commit to check")
            todo = [(s, None) for s in shas]
        elif a.message and not a.commit:
            _staged_index_ok(root)
            with open(a.message, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                message = fh.read()
            head = git(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "HEAD", ok_missing=True)
            if head is None:
                out.append("nmas-red-green: the commit being made is the first: no parent, "
                           "nothing to compare, passes")
                todo = []
            else:
                head = head.strip()
                lines, why = check(root, head, INDEX, message, f"the commit being made (the "
                                   f"index on {head[:12]})", **kw)
                out += lines
                refused += [("the commit being made", w) for w in why]
                todo = []
        else:
            sha = git(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", (a.commit or "HEAD") + "^{commit}").strip()
            todo = [(sha, a.message)]
        for sha, message_file in todo:
            parents = git(root, "log", "-1", "--format=%P", sha).split()
            subject = git(root, "log", "-1", "--format=%s", sha).strip()
            label = f"{sha[:12]} {subject[:80]}"
            if not parents:
                out.append(f"nmas-red-green: {label}: a root commit, no parent to compare, "
                           "passes")
                continue
            if message_file:
                with open(message_file, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                    message = fh.read()
            else:
                message = git(root, "log", "-1", "--format=%B", sha)
            lines, why = check(root, parents[0], sha, message, label, **kw)
            out += lines
            refused += [(sha[:12], w) for w in why]
    except (CannotAsk, OSError) as exc:
        print("\n".join(out), flush=True)
        print(f"nmas-red-green: COULD NOT ASK: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    print("\n".join(out), flush=True)
    for where, why in refused:
        print(f"nmas-red-green: REFUSED: {where} {why}", file=sys.stderr)
    if refused:
        print("nmas-red-green: each new or changed test's own body must fail on the commit's "
              "parent (docs/TEST_INTEGRITY.md, item 1; a setup or collection error is not "
              "red, C2633); one written to hold behaviour that already works says so: "
              "'Guards-Existing: <test id> — <reason>'; a file that cannot import on the parent "
              "a module this commit adds: 'Red-By-Collection: <file> — <reason>'",
              file=sys.stderr)
    return 1 if refused else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
